{"type":"message","id":"4f02ceb7","parentId":"5ca3f829","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:29:09.643Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Sat 2026-05-23 12:29 UTC] [Subagent Context] You are running as a subagent (depth 1/1). Results auto-announce to your requester; do not busy-poll for status.\n\n[Subagent Task]: Generate a feature image for this article. Read /tmp/crypto-run-20260523-122344/research/validated.json (also at /tmp/research.json) to find the article title, topic, and primary crypto asset. Use the Scene Formula in your SOUL.md to pick the right human subject and scene, then run the API calls and logo stamp steps exactly as defined in your SOUL.md."}],"timestamp":1779539349633}}
{"type":"message","id":"70c0bcae","parentId":"4f02ceb7","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:29:11.908Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_dlUzpXo5nnjJgCWkJYk9J3F0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260523-122344/research/validated.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_VuIBL9kv1vkAsSa7tjXKn7sn","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/research.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ICBYuuksnGk9mZ1YHG2N2etA","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-creator/SOUL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4-mini","usage":{"input":5961,"output":227,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":6188,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779539349645,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DiflXuvlB5BSw4OM8P3vev3KGU1eb"}}
{"type":"message","id":"34072dfc","parentId":"70c0bcae","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:29:11.949Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_dlUzpXo5nnjJgCWkJYk9J3F0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"sec-delays-tokenized-stocks-exemption-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"SEC delays its tokenized-stocks innovation exemption as concerns mount over synthetic shares and market fragmentation\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"tokenized stocks\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"SEC Delays Tokenized Stocks Innovation Exemption Amid Concerns: Bloomberg\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Ethereum\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"ethereum\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"BeInCrypto\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368855/sec-delays-tokenized-stocks-innovation-exemption-amid-concerns-bloomberg\",\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/news-analysis/2026/05/22/sec-commissioner-peirce-counters-views-that-crypto-rule-will-foster-synthetic-tokens\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/sec-delays-crypto-stock-tokens-pushback/\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/peirce-excludes-synthetic-tokenized-stocks/\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Decrypt, citing Bloomberg, reported that SEC staff had been preparing an 'innovation exemption' for tokenized stocks as soon as this week, but the release was delayed after feedback from stock-exchange officials and other market participants.\",\n    \"A central concern is whether the framework could permit third-party tokenized shares issued without the underlying company’s consent, which critics say could create complications around dividends, voting rights, and shareholder records.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce pushed back on claims the proposal would authorize synthetic stock tokens, saying the carve-out was intended only for digital representations of the same underlying equity security already available in the secondary market today.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto said the proposed exemption was designed to let crypto firms and DeFi platforms offer blockchain-based stock exposure with 24/7 trading, faster settlement, and fractional ownership while still treating the tokens as securities.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto also noted that tokenized real-world assets have grown to more than $34 billion, up roughly 1,600% in two years, while tokenized equities alone now exceed $1 billion in market value.\",\n    \"Ethereum was identified by BeInCrypto as the leading chain for tokenized real-world assets, with Solana following, giving the story a direct link to major crypto infrastructure rather than a purely TradFi policy debate.\",\n    \"The second BeInCrypto report said Peirce’s clarification effectively favored fully backed tokenized shares over synthetic wrappers, a distinction that matters because many DeFi-native tokenization products rely on derivative-style exposure rather than issuer-approved equity claims.\",\n    \"CoinDesk added that SEC Chair Paul Atkins has tied the agency’s crypto-rulemaking effort to broader market-structure reform and to congressional work such as the Digital Asset Market Clarity Act, meaning the delay could ripple into the wider timetable for U.S. on-chain securities policy.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"SOURCE: Decrypt\\nURL: https://decrypt.co/368855/sec-delays-tokenized-stocks-innovation-exemption-amid-concerns-bloomberg\\n\\nDecrypt reported that the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission has delayed a planned innovation exemption that would have opened the door for crypto firms to offer tokenized versions of stocks and other tokenized assets under a lighter-touch framework. According to Decrypt’s summary of Bloomberg’s reporting, SEC staff had been preparing to publish the exemption as soon as this week, but the timeline slipped after stock-exchange officials and other market participants raised objections in recent discussions with the agency. The article said one of the biggest sticking points is the possibility that the framework could allow trading in third-party tokens, meaning digital versions of company shares issued without the knowledge or approval of the underlying corporation. Critics warned that such instruments could complicate dividends, shareholder voting, and cap-table administration if tokenized claims start circulating outside traditional market plumbing. Decrypt also highlighted Hester Peirce’s response on X, where she said the proposal was narrower than critics were claiming and would facilitate trading only in digital representations of the same underlying equity security an investor could already buy in the secondary market, 'not synthetics.'\\n\\nSOURCE: CoinDesk\\nURL: https://www.coindesk.com/news-analysis/2026/05/22/sec-commissioner-peirce-counters-views-that-crypto-rule-will-foster-synthetic-tokens\\n\\nCoinDesk added political and regulatory context to the story. Its report said the tokenized-stock exemption would be one of the most meaningful crypto-related regulatory changes the SEC has attempted so far because it would create a concrete path for blockchain-based securities trading inside the United States. CoinDesk emphasized that Chairman Paul Atkins has spent months signaling that the SEC is preparing a broader set of crypto safe harbors and exemptions, including relief for startup registration, fundraising, and questions around when digital assets should stop being treated as securities. In that context, the tokenized-stock framework is not an isolated tweak; it sits inside a larger attempt to rewire how crypto and on-chain finance fit into U.S. securities law. CoinDesk also reported that Peirce’s clarification was aimed at cooling down market speculation that the SEC was about to bless synthetic stock products. She said the exemption was intended for digital representations of existing shares, not free-floating synthetic instruments. The report further noted that the SEC and CFTC have both been writing rules while expecting Congress to eventually cement much of the new approach through market-structure legislation such as the Clarity Act. The update on CoinDesk also confirmed that the expected rule release had in fact been delayed.\\n\\nSOURCE: BeInCrypto\\nURL: https://beincrypto.com/sec-delays-crypto-stock-tokens-pushback/\\n\\nBeInCrypto focused on how the proposed exemption could reshape market structure if it eventually moves forward. The outlet said the plan would let crypto platforms and DeFi venues trade blockchain-based versions of stocks such as Apple or Tesla, bringing 24/7 trading, faster settlement, and easier fractional ownership into equity markets while still keeping the tokens inside a securities-law framework. But it said exchange officials and other participants raised alarms that parallel on-chain trading venues could fragment liquidity away from traditional exchanges and weaken price discovery. Investor protection was another major theme: tokenized shares issued without direct company involvement may not carry the same voting rights, dividend rights, or legal clarity as conventional brokerage-held shares. BeInCrypto supplied useful scale on why this matters now. It said tokenized real-world assets have grown to more than $34 billion, up around 1,600% in two years, and that tokenized equities alone have crossed $1 billion in market value. The report also said Ethereum currently leads the tokenized-RWA market, followed by Solana, reinforcing that the SEC’s decision would directly affect major crypto infrastructure and not just abstract policy theory.\\n\\nSOURCE: BeInCrypto\\nURL: https://beincrypto.com/peirce-excludes-synthetic-tokenized-stocks/\\n\\nA second BeInCrypto article drilled deeper into Peirce’s wording and why the market reacted so strongly. It said her clarification effectively excluded synthetic tokenized stocks and limited the contemplated carve-out to digital representations of listed equities that investors can already purchase in the secondary market. The piece explained that synthetic or linked securities typically provide only economic exposure to an underlying share rather than an actual claim on the security itself, which means holders can face counterparty risk and may lose governance rights such as voting or dividend entitlements. BeInCrypto said the debate mattered because many DeFi-native tokenization models rely on wrappers or synthetic designs precisely because they are easier to launch than issuer-sponsored or fully custodial structures. The article cited Galaxy Research’s Alex Thorn as an example of how intensely policy teams and tokenization firms were parsing Peirce’s wording. Its conclusion was that Peirce’s interpretation favors fully backed tokenization experiments over derivative-like stock exposure products.\\n\\nCOMBINED SYNTHESIS\\n\\nTaken together, the four reports point to the same core development: the SEC was preparing a significant tokenized-stocks exemption, but pulled back at the last minute because the hardest questions around synthetic exposure, investor rights, and exchange-market fragmentation are not resolved. That makes this more than a simple scheduling delay. It is a test of how far the U.S. is willing to let blockchain infrastructure penetrate mainstream capital markets. The debate also reveals a split inside tokenization itself. One camp wants fully backed, legally anchored digital representations of real shares. Another wants synthetic or wrapper-based products that are easier to deploy on crypto rails and more composable inside DeFi. Peirce’s remarks suggest the SEC is willing to explore the first path but is not ready to bless the second.\\n\\nThis is a strong crypto story because it sits at the intersection of regulation, tokenization, market structure, and institutional adoption. If the exemption eventually emerges in a narrowed form, it could accelerate on-chain securities trading on networks like Ethereum while excluding some of the most aggressive DeFi-native designs. If the delay drags on, it signals that even a crypto-friendlier SEC still sees tokenized equities as a sensitive area where mistakes could spill into core stock-market functions such as price discovery, settlement, voting, and shareholder accounting. Either way, the reporting shows the tokenized-stocks push has moved from theory into live regulatory combat.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779539351949}}
{"type":"message","id":"185f2eff","parentId":"34072dfc","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:29:11.949Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_VuIBL9kv1vkAsSa7tjXKn7sn","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"sec-delays-tokenized-stocks-exemption-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"SEC delays its tokenized-stocks innovation exemption as concerns mount over synthetic shares and market fragmentation\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"tokenized stocks\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"SEC Delays Tokenized Stocks Innovation Exemption Amid Concerns: Bloomberg\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Ethereum\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"ethereum\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"BeInCrypto\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368855/sec-delays-tokenized-stocks-innovation-exemption-amid-concerns-bloomberg\",\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/news-analysis/2026/05/22/sec-commissioner-peirce-counters-views-that-crypto-rule-will-foster-synthetic-tokens\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/sec-delays-crypto-stock-tokens-pushback/\",\n    \"https://beincrypto.com/peirce-excludes-synthetic-tokenized-stocks/\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Decrypt, citing Bloomberg, reported that SEC staff had been preparing an 'innovation exemption' for tokenized stocks as soon as this week, but the release was delayed after feedback from stock-exchange officials and other market participants.\",\n    \"A central concern is whether the framework could permit third-party tokenized shares issued without the underlying company’s consent, which critics say could create complications around dividends, voting rights, and shareholder records.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce pushed back on claims the proposal would authorize synthetic stock tokens, saying the carve-out was intended only for digital representations of the same underlying equity security already available in the secondary market today.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto said the proposed exemption was designed to let crypto firms and DeFi platforms offer blockchain-based stock exposure with 24/7 trading, faster settlement, and fractional ownership while still treating the tokens as securities.\",\n    \"BeInCrypto also noted that tokenized real-world assets have grown to more than $34 billion, up roughly 1,600% in two years, while tokenized equities alone now exceed $1 billion in market value.\",\n    \"Ethereum was identified by BeInCrypto as the leading chain for tokenized real-world assets, with Solana following, giving the story a direct link to major crypto infrastructure rather than a purely TradFi policy debate.\",\n    \"The second BeInCrypto report said Peirce’s clarification effectively favored fully backed tokenized shares over synthetic wrappers, a distinction that matters because many DeFi-native tokenization products rely on derivative-style exposure rather than issuer-approved equity claims.\",\n    \"CoinDesk added that SEC Chair Paul Atkins has tied the agency’s crypto-rulemaking effort to broader market-structure reform and to congressional work such as the Digital Asset Market Clarity Act, meaning the delay could ripple into the wider timetable for U.S. on-chain securities policy.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"SOURCE: Decrypt\\nURL: https://decrypt.co/368855/sec-delays-tokenized-stocks-innovation-exemption-amid-concerns-bloomberg\\n\\nDecrypt reported that the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission has delayed a planned innovation exemption that would have opened the door for crypto firms to offer tokenized versions of stocks and other tokenized assets under a lighter-touch framework. According to Decrypt’s summary of Bloomberg’s reporting, SEC staff had been preparing to publish the exemption as soon as this week, but the timeline slipped after stock-exchange officials and other market participants raised objections in recent discussions with the agency. The article said one of the biggest sticking points is the possibility that the framework could allow trading in third-party tokens, meaning digital versions of company shares issued without the knowledge or approval of the underlying corporation. Critics warned that such instruments could complicate dividends, shareholder voting, and cap-table administration if tokenized claims start circulating outside traditional market plumbing. Decrypt also highlighted Hester Peirce’s response on X, where she said the proposal was narrower than critics were claiming and would facilitate trading only in digital representations of the same underlying equity security an investor could already buy in the secondary market, 'not synthetics.'\\n\\nSOURCE: CoinDesk\\nURL: https://www.coindesk.com/news-analysis/2026/05/22/sec-commissioner-peirce-counters-views-that-crypto-rule-will-foster-synthetic-tokens\\n\\nCoinDesk added political and regulatory context to the story. Its report said the tokenized-stock exemption would be one of the most meaningful crypto-related regulatory changes the SEC has attempted so far because it would create a concrete path for blockchain-based securities trading inside the United States. CoinDesk emphasized that Chairman Paul Atkins has spent months signaling that the SEC is preparing a broader set of crypto safe harbors and exemptions, including relief for startup registration, fundraising, and questions around when digital assets should stop being treated as securities. In that context, the tokenized-stock framework is not an isolated tweak; it sits inside a larger attempt to rewire how crypto and on-chain finance fit into U.S. securities law. CoinDesk also reported that Peirce’s clarification was aimed at cooling down market speculation that the SEC was about to bless synthetic stock products. She said the exemption was intended for digital representations of existing shares, not free-floating synthetic instruments. The report further noted that the SEC and CFTC have both been writing rules while expecting Congress to eventually cement much of the new approach through market-structure legislation such as the Clarity Act. The update on CoinDesk also confirmed that the expected rule release had in fact been delayed.\\n\\nSOURCE: BeInCrypto\\nURL: https://beincrypto.com/sec-delays-crypto-stock-tokens-pushback/\\n\\nBeInCrypto focused on how the proposed exemption could reshape market structure if it eventually moves forward. The outlet said the plan would let crypto platforms and DeFi venues trade blockchain-based versions of stocks such as Apple or Tesla, bringing 24/7 trading, faster settlement, and easier fractional ownership into equity markets while still keeping the tokens inside a securities-law framework. But it said exchange officials and other participants raised alarms that parallel on-chain trading venues could fragment liquidity away from traditional exchanges and weaken price discovery. Investor protection was another major theme: tokenized shares issued without direct company involvement may not carry the same voting rights, dividend rights, or legal clarity as conventional brokerage-held shares. BeInCrypto supplied useful scale on why this matters now. It said tokenized real-world assets have grown to more than $34 billion, up around 1,600% in two years, and that tokenized equities alone have crossed $1 billion in market value. The report also said Ethereum currently leads the tokenized-RWA market, followed by Solana, reinforcing that the SEC’s decision would directly affect major crypto infrastructure and not just abstract policy theory.\\n\\nSOURCE: BeInCrypto\\nURL: https://beincrypto.com/peirce-excludes-synthetic-tokenized-stocks/\\n\\nA second BeInCrypto article drilled deeper into Peirce’s wording and why the market reacted so strongly. It said her clarification effectively excluded synthetic tokenized stocks and limited the contemplated carve-out to digital representations of listed equities that investors can already purchase in the secondary market. The piece explained that synthetic or linked securities typically provide only economic exposure to an underlying share rather than an actual claim on the security itself, which means holders can face counterparty risk and may lose governance rights such as voting or dividend entitlements. BeInCrypto said the debate mattered because many DeFi-native tokenization models rely on wrappers or synthetic designs precisely because they are easier to launch than issuer-sponsored or fully custodial structures. The article cited Galaxy Research’s Alex Thorn as an example of how intensely policy teams and tokenization firms were parsing Peirce’s wording. Its conclusion was that Peirce’s interpretation favors fully backed tokenization experiments over derivative-like stock exposure products.\\n\\nCOMBINED SYNTHESIS\\n\\nTaken together, the four reports point to the same core development: the SEC was preparing a significant tokenized-stocks exemption, but pulled back at the last minute because the hardest questions around synthetic exposure, investor rights, and exchange-market fragmentation are not resolved. That makes this more than a simple scheduling delay. It is a test of how far the U.S. is willing to let blockchain infrastructure penetrate mainstream capital markets. The debate also reveals a split inside tokenization itself. One camp wants fully backed, legally anchored digital representations of real shares. Another wants synthetic or wrapper-based products that are easier to deploy on crypto rails and more composable inside DeFi. Peirce’s remarks suggest the SEC is willing to explore the first path but is not ready to bless the second.\\n\\nThis is a strong crypto story because it sits at the intersection of regulation, tokenization, market structure, and institutional adoption. If the exemption eventually emerges in a narrowed form, it could accelerate on-chain securities trading on networks like Ethereum while excluding some of the most aggressive DeFi-native designs. If the delay drags on, it signals that even a crypto-friendlier SEC still sees tokenized equities as a sensitive area where mistakes could spill into core stock-market functions such as price discovery, settlement, voting, and shareholder accounting. Either way, the reporting shows the tokenized-stocks push has moved from theory into live regulatory combat.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779539351949}}
{"type":"message","id":"17334780","parentId":"185f2eff","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:29:11.949Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ICBYuuksnGk9mZ1YHG2N2etA","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# SOUL.md — Pixel, the Image Creator\n\nYou are **Pixel** 🎨, an AI image generation specialist for a crypto news publication. You produce one editorial feature image per article using the Leonardo AI API via a hardened bash skill script.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ CRITICAL RULES — READ BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE\n\n1. **You have a `bash` tool. You MUST USE IT.** Do not describe what you would do. Do not output fake file paths. Execute the actual commands below.\n2. **NEVER call the Leonardo AI API directly.** The skill script handles all API calls. Your only job is crafting the prompt and running the script.\n3. **NEVER invent or guess a file path.** The only valid success output is what `cat /tmp/image-result.txt` prints after the script exits 0.\n4. **Every step marked `[TOOL CALL REQUIRED]` must produce a real bash tool call.** No exceptions.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Craft the Image Prompt\n\n<thinking>\nLook at the article title and topic. Match it to the closest Scene Template below. Pick the scene. Write the prompt following the formula exactly. Keep it under 300 characters. End with the required style suffix.\n</thinking>\n\n### The Formula (ALWAYS follow this)\n> [A real human subject doing/reacting to the story] + [specific crypto asset visually present in the scene] + editorial photography style\n\n### Hard Rules\n- **MUST feature at least one human being** — a trader, developer, executive, regulator, investor. Never zero people. NON-NEGOTIABLE.\n- **MUST include the relevant crypto asset visually** — Bitcoin logo on a screen, Ethereum chart on a monitor, DeFi interface visible on a browser.\n- **Prefer medium shot (waist-up or over-shoulder)** — monitors in background. Do NOT request extreme close-ups of face or hands on keyboard (anatomy failures).\n- Describe a real candid moment: what is the person doing, where are they, what is on their screen.\n- **End ALL prompts with exactly:** `editorial press photography, Reuters style, photorealistic, DSLR, 35mm lens, sharp focus, candid`\n- **NO TEXT in the prompt** — do not describe words, signs, labels, or numbers that should appear IN the image.\n- Do NOT describe circuit boards, abstract glowing shapes, or floating coins with no humans.\n- Keep the prompt under 300 characters.\n\nThe skill script uses **Leonardo PhotoReal v2 + STOCK_PHOTO** on Vision XL. Your prompt should describe the **scene and subject** only; do not add \"creative illustration\" or \"digital art\" wording.\n\n### Scene Templates (pick closest match)\n\n| Story Type | Scene to Describe |\n|---|---|\n| Price movement (BTC/ETH up or down) | Medium shot of trader at multi-monitor desk, Bitcoin chart glowing on background screen, trading floor |\n| Regulation / government ban | Government official at podium or signing desk, cryptocurrency logos on projected screen |\n| Fork / protocol upgrade / developer news | Software developer at laptop in office, blockchain interface visible on screen |\n| ETF / institutional flows / investment | Financial analyst at Bloomberg terminal, Bitcoin or Ethereum ticker on display |\n| DeFi exploit / hack / security breach | Cybersecurity professional at dark workstation, Ethereum or DeFi dashboard visible on screen |\n| Exchange / trading platform news | Crypto exchange trader at desk, trading platform UI on monitor |\n| General crypto market | Investor looking at phone with crypto portfolio app, office or coffee shop background |\n\n### Universal Fallback\nIf unsure which template to use, ALWAYS default to:\n> `Medium shot of focused male trader at multi-screen desk, Bitcoin chart on background monitor, dark professional office, editorial press photography, Reuters style, photorealistic, DSLR, 35mm lens, sharp focus, candid`\n\n---\n\n## Step 2 — Execute the Skill Script `[TOOL CALL REQUIRED]`\n\n<thinking>\nI have crafted my prompt. I will now execute the bash tool. I will NOT output the result — I will wait for the actual exit code from the script.\n</thinking>\n\nRun this **exact** command using your bash tool. Replace `<YOUR CRAFTED PROMPT>` with the prompt you crafted in Step 1:\n\n```bash\nbash ~/.openclaw/workspace-creator/skills/generate-image/generate.sh \"<YOUR CRAFTED PROMPT>\"\n```\n\n**WAIT for the script to finish. Do NOT skip this step. Do NOT guess the output.**\n\nThe script will handle everything: API submission, retries, polling (up to 60 seconds), image download, JPEG validation, and logo watermarking.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3 — Verify Output File `[TOOL CALL REQUIRED]`\n\n<thinking>\nThe script has exited. Sometimes the script exits with an error (like a failed logo stamp), but the image is actually perfectly fine! I MUST check the file on disk before deciding if I failed.\n</thinking>\n\nRun this bash command to verify the final file (resolves symlinks — pipeline uses `/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg` → run dir):\n```bash\nFEATURE_IMG=\"$(readlink -f /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg 2>/dev/null || echo /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg)\"\nif [ -f \"$FEATURE_IMG\" ] && [ $(stat -c%s \"$FEATURE_IMG\" 2>/dev/null || stat -f%z \"$FEATURE_IMG\" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) -gt 10000 ] && file -b \"$FEATURE_IMG\" | grep -qi \"JPEG\\|image\"; then\n  echo \"VALID_IMAGE\"\nelse\n  echo \"INVALID_IMAGE\"\nfi\n```\n\n---\n\n## Step 4 — Final Output\n\nIf the Step 3 bash command outputs `VALID_IMAGE`:\nReturn exactly: `/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg`\n\nIf the Step 3 bash command outputs `INVALID_IMAGE`:\nRun `cat /tmp/image-error.log` to see what went wrong, and then return exactly:\n`IMAGE_FAILED: <contents of error log>`\n\n**No commentary. No explanation. No apology. Just the result.**\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779539351949}}
